52
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,000
So in this podcast we're going to talk a little bit about the difference

53
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:16,000
between planning and preparation in Scout Master Ship in seven minutes or less.

54
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Then we've got an email question to answer.

55
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
That's going to take care of the rest of the podcast.

56
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
so let's go ahead and get started, shall we

57
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Scout Master Ship in seven minutes or less?

58
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
So the Patrol Leaders Council sits down,

59
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:36,000
They plan.

60
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Then they get up from the table and they have their plans written down

61
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,000
And the assumption is that the Scouts are going to start preparing.

62
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,000
They'll go home and they'll pour over their Scout Handbook

63
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:52,000
and, you know, take notes and look things up online

64
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:56,000
and spend a whole lot of time preparing for that next meeting,

65
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Getting things ready right.

66
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,000
Then we get to the next meeting and we find out,

67
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
oh, they really didn't do too much of that, did they?

68
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
So I'm going to mention this again.

69
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,000
I think I mentioned it recently,

70
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:15,000
But we've started to differentiate between the idea of planning

71
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,000
and preparation in our Patrol Leaders Council meetings

72
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
And we do it this way:

73
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,000
The planning part, that's easy, okay,

74
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
That's who's going to do what and when they're going to do it

75
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
and what day it's going to happen.

76
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
That's a piece of cake.

77
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,000
You need 10 minutes to do that for a month's full of meetings really.

78
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
The preparation part is usually where everything falls apart,

79
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
because they don't do this in the context

80
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
of a Patrol Leaders Council meeting. necessarily.

81
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
They're sitting around a table somewhere,

82
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
They're coming up with a plan and then they're talking

83
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
about the television show that they saw last night

84
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
or the video game that they're playing,

85
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
And then they work on the plan a little bit more

86
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
and then they switch back to something else.

87
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
And you know, remember being 14 or 15?

88
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
You know, if you thought of something, it was planned,

89
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Basically planning at the speed of thought.

90
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:10,000
So what we're missing sometimes, I think,

91
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
in this youth-led paradigm

92
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,000
where the Scouts are planning and presenting everything,

93
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,000
where they're thinking things through,

94
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
is the fact that they don't really think things through.

95
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
So if I have a 14- or 15-year-old Scout

96
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:31,000
preparing to instruct his troop or Patrol on orienteering,

97
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
he'll think this.

98
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
He'll think I'll be instructing everybody

99
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
about how to do orienteering.

100
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
I've done orienteering in the past,

101
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,000
I have a compass and I'll find a map or something

102
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
and we're ready to go.

103
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
What more do I need to do

104
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
When it comes time to demonstrate

105
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
any skill associated with orienteering

106
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
and instruct others.

107
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
out comes the compass and the map

108
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
and he's going to stand there

109
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,000
and the other Scouts are going to look on

110
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
and he's going to try and remember how to orient a map

111
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,000
and he maybe really hasn't understood what he's doing.

112
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,000
and he opens his handbook

113
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,000
and he's reading the instruction

114
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,000
and in the meantime the dozen Scouts

115
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
that he's trying to work with,

116
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
their attention is elsewhere.

117
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
They're talking or asking questions,

118
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
They're bored.

119
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
The whole thing devolves into that familiar chaos

120
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
we all know so well

121
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
because they didn't do much of anything.

122
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:27,000
Now, any adult looking on

123
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
is going to have an almost uncontrollable urge

124
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,000
to step in and save the Scout

125
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
and ask for the compass and the map

126
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
and show everybody how to do this themselves.

127
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
We're going to decide that this whole

128
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
boy-led idea

129
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
and this idea of Scouts instructing

130
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
and planning things

131
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
Well, this just doesn't work.

132
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
So we're going to take over

133
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
and we can avoid this, I think,

134
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
by being very intentional

135
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
about helping our Scouts get through

136
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,000
the preparation stage.

137
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
Now, you don't need to do this every single time

138
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
a Scout is preparing something,

139
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
but think of it as a little bit of that

140
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
on-the-job training,

141
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
that is a little bit of mentoring

142
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
We need to lead them to discover

143
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
how to prepare

144
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
and how to instruct. well,

145
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
by asking questions.

146
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
So here's an example of what I'm talking about.

147
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
Let's say the Patrol Leaders Council meeting

148
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
is over. It was fairly simple.

149
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
It took 10, 15, 20 minutes maybe,

150
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
And they established

151
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
the structure and the content

152
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
and the plan for their activities.

153
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
And now it's time to prepare.

154
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Using my example,

155
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
I'm going to be instructing his fellow Scouts

156
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
on orienting a map,

157
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
And he sits down with me

158
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
so I can coach him through the preparation process.

159
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
And I'll do this

160
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
by asking him a question.

161
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
So what are you going to need to make this happen?

162
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
And he'll say:

163
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
do you have one?

164
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
And

165
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
that's the point where he'll probably say,

166
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
yeah, I don't know,

167
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
Maybe we have one somewhere

168
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
And maybe we spend

169
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
a few minutes finding one

170
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
in the Scout Closet or something.

171
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
And then

172
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
I would ask-

173
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
let's say it's next week

174
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
and you're the instructor

175
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
and I'm a Scout- show me what happens next.

176
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
And my Scout

177
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
is going to kind of stand there for a second

178
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
and he's going to say:

179
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
well, I'm not sure

180
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
what I'm going to do next.

181
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
And I said: well,

182
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
you need to show me how to orient a map.

183
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
And well, okay, well, here's the compass

184
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
and the map and you do this.

185
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
Wait a minute. wait a minute,

186
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
I know how to do this.

187
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
I'm going to ask him the question:

188
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
what are the Scouts doing at this point?

189
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
And he's probably going to tell me that they have

190
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
pretty much so lost interest

191
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
and gone right through the scenario.

192
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
So I'm going to walk him through this

193
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
question wise.

194
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
Hey, where is it written down about how to orient a map?

195
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
Well, maybe it's in the Scout Handbook.

196
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Hey, do we have one of those? Yeah,

197
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Alright, so let's see what we can do here

198
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
Now. are you going to remember all this

199
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
when you're getting ready to do this next time?

200
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
Oh sure, Yeah,

201
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
I said. well, is there any way you can kind of

202
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
store this in a memory somewhere

203
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
so that you will actually remember it?

204
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
Yeah, well, maybe I could write some of it down.

205
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
Okay, so now,

206
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
in the space of

207
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
maybe 15 or 20 minutes,

208
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
I've asked a lot of questions

209
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
and now he has, like a reasonably good

210
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
handle on what he needs to do.

211
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
He's beginning to work things out.

212
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
Maybe he's even taking a few notes

213
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
and writing things down. you know,

214
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
I didn't tell him anything,

215
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
I just asked questions

216
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
and I helped him discover things for himself.

217
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
And I think in doing this

218
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
we've not only helped him

219
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
through that particular process.

220
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
we've been

221
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
starting to impart a skill to him

222
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
that he's not likely going to forget.

223
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
Once a scout's been

224
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
coached through that process a couple of times,

225
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
he'll be a self-starter.

226
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
He'll anticipate and answer the questions

227
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
for himself,

228
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
And when he's instructing his fellow scouts

229
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
things won't decay into chaos

230
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
maybe quite so quickly.

231
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
They'll just be that low roar of activity

232
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
and learning that looks like chaos

233
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
to the adult eye. We could, in theory,

234
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
take all of our older scouts and set up

235
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
a how to instruct training session,

236
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
but I like the idea of

237
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
mentoring with something that is

238
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
actually related

239
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
to something he needs to do.

240
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
right then, If we just set up

241
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
that kind of how to instruct training session

242
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
and do that once a year or something,

243
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
that's just another adult

244
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
talking to him about things that they don't really attach

245
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
to any kind of actual

246
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
need to know. If we coach

247
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
and mentor them with an actual

248
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
rather than theoretical task in mind,

249
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
there's a sense of utility

250
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
about that coaching that makes

251
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
it more valuable to them and actually

252
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
helps them to learn a new skill.

253
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
So think about

254
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
separating planning and preparation.

255
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
Think about pointing out the

256
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
importance of good preparation.

257
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
Ask questions and coach a scout

258
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
through the process of preparation

259
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
And pretty soon he'll be able to do it

260
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
all by himself.

261
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
Write me a letter,

262
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
send it by name

263
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
Email. that is, folks.

264
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
And here's an answer to one of your

265
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
emails.

266
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
I heard from Carl Smith,

267
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
who is with Troupe 603

268
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
out in Cave Creek, Arizona,

269
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
and he asked me this question.

270
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
he says our troop has for many years

271
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
assimilated new scouts like

272
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
Weeblos who cross over into the pack

273
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
by putting them in their own new patrol

274
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
and because of that our patrols

275
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
are pretty much divided by age.

276
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
There are some benefits to this type

277
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
of patrol structure, but several

278
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
of the assistant scout masters feel the boys

279
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
are missing out on one of the most

280
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
important aspects of scouting:

281
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
older boys leading younger boys.

282
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
We have a buy-in from our current

283
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
scout master to reorganize patrol

284
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
so that each patrol has a strong mix

285
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
of older and younger boys,

286
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
but he wants a detailed

287
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
plan on how we will both execute

288
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
this as well as handle some of the

289
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
potential rough spots.

290
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
We have a troop of about 80 boys.

291
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
about 35% of them

292
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
are older and about 50%

293
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
of them are younger,

294
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
So I'm looking for some advice.

295
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
What's the best way to reassign

296
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
boys to new patrols.

297
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
Should we make sure to put a NYLT

298
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
trained boy in each patrol?

299
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
Should we distribute the reliable older boys

300
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
amongst the patrols?

301
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
Number two is many of the patrols

302
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
have been together for years.

303
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
We are making this change.

304
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
Number three is several of the older boys

305
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
only attend meetings sporadically.

306
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
How do we handle the situation where a patrol

307
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
has none of its older boys there

308
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
to lead the younger boys?

309
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
And number four: because of this change,

310
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
we feel the new patrols will need some

311
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
guidance while they learn

312
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
the ropes. How should we do this?

313
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
Remember we have 10 plus patrols

314
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
and we do not have that many

315
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
assistant scout masters.

316
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
Any other guidance or advice would be:

317
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
Carl, I wouldn't recommend

318
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
changing your patrols

319
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
around

320
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
like you're suggesting,

321
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
unless the patrol leaders council says

322
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
that they think it changes in order.

323
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
After all, it is their troop and they ought

324
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
to be able to figure things out like this

325
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
for themselves. As a scout master

326
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
with our troop, I don't have anything

327
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
to do with who is in what patrol.

328
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
I don't assign scouts to patrols

329
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
and frankly, I don't really care

330
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
if a patrol has 10 members or 4

331
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
or what their ages are.

332
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Every once in a while,

333
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
the senior patrol leader will come to me

334
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
for advice on handling a patrol

335
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
or maybe resetting all the patrols in the troop,

336
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
And I always tell him the best thing

337
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
to do is to get together with the scouts

338
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
and let them decide who goes where.

339
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
If he runs into a problem

340
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
he can always ask my advice. but

341
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
I don't really get that all that often.

342
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
This works well for us because

343
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
basically we understand that boys join

344
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
scouting to be with their friends

345
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
on camping trips and at patrol and troop meetings.

346
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
They make up patrols without

347
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
a whole lot of fuss and bother

348
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
and when we have a new group joined

349
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
from Weeblos usually they choose

350
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
to stay together more often than not

351
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
and they may spend the whole of their years

352
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
in the troop in the same patrol together.

353
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
Most older boys aren't interested

354
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
in being in a patrol with younger boys

355
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
now. they're happy to instruct them

356
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
and to help out, but they really

357
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
want to be in a patrol with their friends.

358
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
I'm not interested in coercing

359
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
them to lead. I asked

360
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
if they would care to

361
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
and the ones who are interested

362
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
say yes and the others

363
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
do what they can to help and somehow

364
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
it all works out. Now you sent me this question

365
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
just after we welcomed a new

366
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
group of Weeblos into our troop.

367
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
My two junior assistant scout masters

368
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
agreed to work with them for the next few months

369
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
to get them going. They all elect

370
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
a new patrol leader every month so that

371
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
they can learn a little bit about how a patrol works.

372
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
I asked these junior assistant scout masters

373
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
to work with these, these scouts,

374
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
with the idea that by the time summer camp

375
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
comes around they'll be functioning as

376
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
a patrol on their own. I'll have

377
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
an 11 year old patrol leader and he'll do

378
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
just fine. We've had many others

379
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
who did pretty well too. As for

380
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
adults- kind of helping patrols

381
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
out, I don't let adults get anywhere

382
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
near patrols. as a rule,

383
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
Boys automatically defer to adults

384
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
when they are around and the adults

385
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
end up leading more than the scouts.

386
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
When we're meeting or camping,

387
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
our adults are a respectable

388
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
distance away from scouts, observing

389
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
rather than participating in the activity.

390
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
We are there if they are

391
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
needed, but they don't seem

392
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
to need us very much. to tell you the truth,

393
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
Patrols camp in their own area.

394
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
they cook their own food. they looked after

395
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
each other. Many times I've been on a

396
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
camp out without ever going into the

397
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
patrol areas at all. So all this

398
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
goes to point out that scouts

399
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
can form their own patrols, elect

400
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
their own leaders and do just about everything

401
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
short of writing checks and driving themselves

402
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
back and forth to camping trips.

403
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
The fewer things we do to interrupt

404
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
or interfere with that process,

405
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
the more capable they become

406
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
at doing it for themselves. I sent

407
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
that answer off and Carl wrote back

408
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
and he said: that's an awesome reply,

409
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
exceptionally insightful. I hope you don't mind

410
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
if I share your reply with some of our

411
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
assistant scout masters and our scout master.

412
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
I have only one thing to add:

413
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
nearly everything you pointed out that

414
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:23,000
scouts should be doing we

415
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
currently have adults doing, and that's a

416
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
big problem. Many of that

417
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,000
established guard believes

418
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
that we have a well running troop

419
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
and they're quite resistant to changing

420
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
anything. It runs well,

421
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
of course, because the adults are running

422
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
much of it, But I believe we need to change.

423
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
maybe we should start somewhere else

424
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
and leave the patrols alone for now.

425
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
As you may imagine, Carl,

426
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
I couldn't agree with you more Obviously.

427
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
you're doing something right with a troop of

428
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
80 plus boys. Now maybe the thing

429
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
to do is to look at the way

430
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
that things are being run

431
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
and see if, by applying

432
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
the patrol method- and that'll begin with

433
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
some discussions with the adult

434
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
leadership, probably returning to a lot

435
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
of the things that you've heard through training

436
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,000
and things like that.

437
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
You get the scout master handbook out

438
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
and the boy scout handbook and

439
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
especially the senior

440
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
patrol leader handbook. That's kind of a

441
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
rare item but you can find one at

442
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
your scout shop and I'd even

443
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
suggest working the patrol method. It's

444
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
a book that you'll find on our website.

445
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
Once you have a commitment out of the adults

446
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
to make this change, it'll take a little while.

447
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
there'll be some chaotic times

448
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
and there'll be times where you're pretty sure it's

449
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
not going to work. But in the end,

450
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
if you stay committed to it

451
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
and you are

452
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
determined to make it happen, it will

453
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
work. Thanks for being in touch, Carl.

